ive thinking where things are  much more specifically defined  whereas more modern application  development relies on  a declarative approach previously in  enterprises  applications once they're developed they  go through a security evaluation process  and that is how they get approved for  deployment and that usually comes  towards the  end of the software development life  cycle what that means is it becomes a  choice between fixing the security  issues  and going into production enterprises  are looking to change that and  incorporate security into every phase of  their software development life cycle  increasingly companies are also starting  to adopt open source software  and that is causing them to rethink how  they  test as well as deploy software  the typical governance models which i in  use within organizations  were created for the on-premise work and  those have not really kept up with the  kind of  rapid deployments which you see in  companies who have adopted public cloud  and those also need to change  devops research and assessment is an  organization which  does a research on these topics and this  is a company which  google acquired a little while ago  they published a report in 2019 after  talking to a lot of enterprises about  the state of  uh devops in those respective  organizations and they had some key  findings  the first one was delivering software  quickly reliably  and safely is at the heart of technology  transformation and it really affects the  organization performance  most companies come from the on-prem  world and they  bring that approach to public cloud as  well  and that is not conducive to optimum  performance  so how you implement your cloud  infrastructure really matters  the other one is the change approval  process which are prevalent in  enterprises and those can be pretty  antiquated which uh  goes through many uh hoops uh before you  can get approved to  deploy software and uh that has to be  reduced so that it can you can cater to  the modern  development approaches which are  prevalent today  so when the dora team looked at the  organizations and tried to evaluate them  they evaluated them on  four key parameter meters the first one  is the lead time  required for deployment uh the second  one is how  often can you deploy the third one  is how quickly you can recover uh and  change in case of a failure  and the last one was what is the time  required to restore  in case of a issue  so they broke these metrics into uh  different categories and based on these  parameters they classified organizations  into elite high medium and low and if  you look at the elite  organizations they uh they have  on-demand deployments where they can do  multiple deployments per day  and they have a change failure rate of  less than 15  and when you come to the low performing  organizations they deploy maybe once a  month or in the worst case scenario once  every six months  and they have change failure rates which  are really bad  but if you look at the majority of the  organizations they fall in the middle  around 67 percent of organizations they  evaluated  fall in the high or medium category but  all these organizations have a desire to  shift left and become more elite in  their performance  so this is where developing with anthos  comes in because anthos was  built from the ground up uh to cater to  these kind of requirements  so athos was a result of 15 years of  google's innovation in this area  right from 2003 when google engineers  created borg  which again became open sourced as  kubernetes all the way up to 2019  when anthos was released  so let's see how antos enables cloud  native development  today the first aspect which a lot of  our customers are looking at  is a security uh they want separation of  duties so that the developers  and the activities they do are separated  from the activities which the operators  and deployment folks do  uh they want effective vulnerability  management 