rm agnostic  so that when the  company decides to move from one  platform to the other they do not have  to come back and change something  they also want the option to move to a  zero ops mod  on the operator side of the house they  really want declarative platform  management  uh organizations which have moved to uh  public cloud  have uh used things like terraform to  automate  and uh make it more declarative to  create the infrastructure  so they want that same out of their  application platforms as well  they want to be able to apply common  policies across enterprise so that they  can standardize  on how they operate their platforms uh  they want uh  higher visibility into microservices  because enterprises are increasingly  adopting microservices  and it is vital to them to understand  the information behind those  and probably most importantly most as  most organizations are starting to  adopt public cloud they are looking for  ways to  uh limit their operational cost and that  can only happen if you can have a  unified control plane  across on-prem as well as public cloud  so this is where kf comes in kf was  developed uh  by google from the ground up so what it  allows the developers to  do is uh use kf in the same manner they  would use  uh cf commands or the cloud foundry  commands uh they can issue a kf command  which will interact with all the  underlying components of anthos  to provide the same experience which the  developers would have  when they use a cloud foundry uh  operators on the other hand can  use all the declarative approaches  provided by crypto and all the  kubernetes tools  to manage the platform  so what is care so the kf is meant to  uh provide developers the cloud foundry  experience while empowering  operators to adopt declarative practices  we allow uh organizations to move their  cloud foundry applications to anthos  without causing any disruption to their  developers  kf is built by google uh from the ground  up natively on kubernetes  uh we do not use any of the cloud  foundry components uh so that means that  the  organizations no longer have to pay the  subscription or licensing cost  behind cloud foundry say implementation  like for pivotal  and administrators can operate the  platform  using kubect and uh below are listed  some of the commands which are supported  as part of cf  so when we built a kf  we actually brought the best of pcf to  anthos  we ensure we have provide a cf-like  developer workflow  we wanted to make sure that we are  building it on natively on top of  kubernetes so that we remove any of the  dependencies from cloud foundry  uh build packs are a common way uh how  enterprises standardize how their  images are built and deployed so it is  very important to  organizations which use cloud foundry  that we provide support for buildbacks  and that is available as part of kf and  we looked at the commonly used commands  from enterprises  and we added support for 40 plus  commands and we also added support for  service brokers  and service brokers are the mechanisms  which cf use  cf applications use to communicate with  external resources  uh technical support of kfo kf is  provided through all google cloud  support channels  there is no additional cost to using kf  kf support is included as part of your  antho subscription  it is supported both on prem as well as  on gcp and other public clouds  and standard google cloud application  policies apply  look at a migration uh from pcf  uh to anthos so on the left-hand side in  phase one you see  the up the enterprise uh status as of  today  right where they have cf applications as  well as kubernetes  application and their platform team is  maintaining two different platforms one  is cloud foundry  another is probably a container platform  like kubernetes  so that means that the operational  overhead is high  for the organization in phase two when  they start adopting antos and kf  now they have a single platform to  maintain which is anthos  the operators running a cf application  can use kf and the folks running  kubernetes applications can  deploy their applications on a cloud run  rgk  or google kubernetes engine as the  organization develops a comfort level  with anthos and gke  and cloud run they can slowly start  migrating their cloud foundry  applications  from using kf to running natively on  cloud run or gke and we do not mandate  that  organizations do that within a certain  amount of time they can continue to use  kf until  their level reach the level of comfort  where they can migrate their  applications over  and in phase four uh you see the  enterprise has completely moved over  from uh cloud foundry applications to  kubernetes application  and they're using cloud ram and gke  let's look uh now through the various uh  phases of a pcf migration  so there are multiple parts where you  can migrate  from pcf to others and we provide  different pathways for it  for enterprises no enterprises are built  the same and everybody has their own  challenges so we acknowledge it  so certain enterprises feel very  comfortable moving uh straight away from  pcf uh to uh something like cloudrun or  gke  uh either on anthos on-prem or on  gcp which is managed by google but other  organizations have their uh challenges  because migrating  hundreds of apps built on cloud foundry  it's not that simple  uh so they can migrate to anthones and  use kf  uh which will effectively simplify the  operation because they have only one  platform to manage  and continue on on that pathway of  running the cloud foundry applications  until they feel comfortable moving to  cloud.gke  uh the first phase of moving your cloud  foundry application  from cloud foundry to anthos is running  an assessment  so as part of the kf tool we provide an  assessment capability  uh you can run the kf tool against your  cloud foundry application  and it will actually tell you what if it  is a good fit for  the kf platform so in the next slide you  actually  see a sample report which is generated  by the kf2  and on the right hand side it's actually  showing whether  the migration assessment has passed or  not the next stage of using kf to  migrate your applications is now  uh to deploy those applications yeah and  you can do that through  the simple cli of kf and kf push  and you can migrate your existing  applications to anthos  uh the screen shows an example of  deploying  one of the very popular applications  which are used to  demonstrate the capabilities of cloud  foundry it's called spring music and  in this case we are deploying that  application  using kf and the developer experience  will be  pretty much uh similar uh to what they  will see with cloud foundry  once you have deployed the application  now you get to the portion where you're  operating it  uh so the developers can use the various  command line uh  arguments which are required uh which  are available to operate the  applications which they move from pcf to  kf  and operators can use the kf  installation  with the gke and anthony's tools to  manage the deployments  so here you see examples of some of the  kf commands developers can use  to look at their applications they can  use kf scale to scale out their  applications they can use kf logs  to look at the logs of generated by  their application  and operators can use  the various uis which are available to  manage gke  and cloud run to actually see what the  status of our application is  it provides them a single pane of glass  for managing  both their configuration and policies  both on-prem and  in the cloud so they can look at their  configuration and policies  as well as get deep visibility into your  applications through services and  networking  and your call to action today is please  review the resources which are linked as  part of the session  if you are interested in learning more  about our pcf migration strategy  please reach out to your account 